As the coefficient of $x$ is negative, it is moving along +ve $x$-axis and equating the equation

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{y}} & =2.5 \cos \left[\left(2 \pi \times 10^6\right) \mathrm{t}-\left(\pi \times 10^{-2}\right) \mathrm{x}\right] \\
\text { with } \mathrm{y} & =\mathrm{A} \cos (\omega \mathrm{t}-\mathrm{kx}) \\
\omega & =2 \pi \times 106 \\
\Rightarrow \mathrm{f} & =\frac{\omega}{2 \pi}=10^6 \mathrm{~Hz} \\
\mathrm{k} & =\pi \times 10^{-2} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \lambda & =\frac{2 \pi}{\mathrm{k}} \\
& =\frac{2 \pi}{\pi \times 10^{-2}}=200 \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}

Velocity of electromagnetic radiation is the velocity of light (c), ie,

c=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\mu_0 \varepsilon_0}}

where $\mu_0$ is the permeability and $\varepsilon_0$ is the permittivity of free space.

\begin{aligned}
& : \lambda_{\min }=\frac{h c}{e V}=\frac{1.242 \times 10^{-6}}{V} \\
& V=\frac{1.242 \times 10^{-6}}{10^{-11}}=124.2 \mathrm{kV}
\end{aligned}

The accelerating voltage for electrons in X-ray machine should be greater than $124.2 \mathrm{kV}$.

Relation for velocity of light $(c)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{\varepsilon_0 \mu_0}}$ (where $\varepsilon_0=$ permittivity constant equal to $8.85 \times 10^{-12} \mathrm{~F} / \mathrm{m}$ and $\mu_0=$ permeability constant equal to $1.26 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{H} / \mathrm{m}$.
Numerically velocity of light $(c)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{\varepsilon_0 \mu_0}}$
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{\left(8.85 \times 10^{-12}\right) \times\left(1.26 \times 10^{-6}\right)}} \approx 3 \times 10^8 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s} \text {. }
